      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Research indicates no inherent link between non-standard vernaculars like AAVE or heavy regional dialects and lower performance on standardized tests; students who code-switch to standard English during testing and instruction consistently demonstrate equivalent or superior achievement levels. Studies show that cognitive discipline and academic readiness are better predicted by factors such as socioeconomic support, parental engagement, and exposure to educational resources rather than a student's native linguistic accent. Consequently, admissions boards should focus on evaluating a student's capacity to master the specific disciplinary language of the curriculum, as they are already highly proficient in their own communicative dialects in daily life.",
